A field read off a `dyn` asserts to the one struct that declares the field,
since Go's `any` has none. A library struct a handler names -- here the
`error` behind a boundary's `@error(e)` -- joins the package's structs too, and
it declares `message`, so `node.message` found two claimants and was emitted
bare: `node.Message` on an `any`, which neither Go target compiles. The
program's own structs are asked first.

It was `InputEvent` and `value` until an event's parameters stopped being
payload structs; `error` is the one a handler is still handed.

A recursion over state keeps `node` a dyn to codegen, as examples/showcase's
TreeView does; over a constant, or spliced, the read folds.

deny out/go/bubbletea/model.go `[^)]node\.Message` -- the dyn read asserts to Item before selecting
deny out/go/fyne/model.go `[^)]node\.Message` -- the dyn read asserts to Item before selecting
-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"

output {
    go {
        bubbletea
        fyne
    }
}

struct Item {
    message string = ""
    next    dyn = null
}

component show(node dyn = null) node {
    text(value=node.message)
    if node.next != null {
        show(node=node.next)
    }
}

window("t") {
    var seen = ""
    var tree = Item{message="a", next=Item{message="b"}}
    boundary(@error(e) { seen = e.message }) {
        text(value=seen)
    }
    show(node=tree)
}
